#: src/reference/reference/tab-completion-parser.md:143
msgid "Controlling tab completion"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/tab-completion-parser.md:145
msgid ""
"Most parsers have reasonable default tab completion behavior. For example, "
"the string and character literal parsers will suggest the underlying literal "
"for an empty input string. However, it is impractical to determine the valid "
"completions for `charClass`, since it accepts an arbitrary predicate. The "
"`examples` method defines explicit completions for such a parser:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/tab-completion-parser.md:153
msgid "\"0\""
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/tab-completion-parser.md:153
msgid "\"1\""
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/tab-completion-parser.md:153
msgid "\"2\""
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/tab-completion-parser.md:156
msgid ""
"Tab completion will use the examples as suggestions. The other method "
"controlling tab completion is `token`. The main purpose of `token` is to "
"determine the boundaries for suggestions. For example, if your parser is:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/tab-completion-parser.md:165
msgid ""
"then the potential completions on empty input are: `console fg green fg blue "
"bg green bg blue`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/tab-completion-parser.md:168
msgid ""
"Typically, you want to suggest smaller segments or the number of suggestions "
"becomes unmanageable. A better parser is:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/tab-completion-parser.md:175
msgid ""
"Now, the initial suggestions would be (with `_` representing a space): "
"`console fg_ bg_`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/tab-completion-parser.md:178
msgid ""
"Be careful not to overlap or nest tokens, as in `token(\"green\" ~ "
"token(\"blue\"))`. The behavior is unspecified (and should generate an error "
"in the future), but typically the outer most token definition will be used."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/tab-completion-parser.md:184
msgid "Dependent parsers"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/tab-completion-parser.md:186
msgid ""
"Sometimes a parser must analyze some data and then more data needs to be "
"parsed, and it is dependent on the previous one. The key for obtaining this "
"behaviour is to use the `flatMap` function."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/tab-completion-parser.md:190
msgid ""
"As an example, it will shown how to select several items from a list of "
"valid ones with completion, but no duplicates are possible.  A space is used "
"to separate the different items."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/tab-completion-parser.md:207
msgid ""
"As you can see, the `flatMap` function provides the previous value.  With "
"this info, a new parser is constructed for the remaining items.  The `map` "
"combinator is also used in order to transform the output of the parser."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reference/tab-completion-parser.md:211
msgid ""
"The parser is called recursively, until it is found the trivial case of no "
"possible choices."
msgstr ""
